22.6117L16.996 14.8432L23.7872 10.0456C23.9206 9.95133 24 9.7981 24 9.63469Z"></path></defs><use clip-path="url(#clip_RatingStar_undefined-1small)" href="#path_RatingStar_undefined-1small" class="RatingStar__backgroundFill"></use><path class="RatingStar__fill"></path></svg></span></span></div><span class="Text Text__body3"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/show/5593162921">August 9, 2024</a></span></section><section class="ReviewText"><section class="ReviewText__content" dir="auto"><div class="TruncatedContent" tabindex="-1"><div class="TruncatedContent__text TruncatedContent__text--large" tabindex="-1" data-testid="contentContainer"><span class="Formatted"><b>2.5/5</b><br><br><i>The review contains spoilers!</i><br><br>It started out great, but then it took a downturn. I feel bad for giving it such a low rating, especially after waiting almost two years for this book, but I just didn’t enjoy it as much as I thought I would. There were two main factors that influenced my rating — one of which is due to personal preference and might not be an issue for everyone, so I sincerely hope that I will be in the minority with this rating.<br><br><br>But, moving to the point — my thoughts. I’ll start with the positive sides here.<br><br>Callum, aka the Archdemon, Elder Warrior of the Onyx Stone Order, Defender of the High City, Prince of the Nine Circles, guardian of House Laverne and Magni Deicide. As you can see, he has quite a lot of titles, just as he has a lot of good, even amazing qualities. I liked this man, okay. He had one preference that I hate in my heroes, but he was endearing enough for me to overlook it and finish the book. Which is rare, as usually if a character doesn’t have issues with such things, it leads to me putting the book down. <b>So, what made me love him?</b> <br><br>His nicknames.<br><br><blockquote>
  <i>
    <b>"lady witch."<br>"my lady"<br>“My beautiful thread of fate. My lady. My mistress.”<br>"darling."<br>“Yes, my love."<br></b>
  </i>
</blockquote><br><br> ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ <img src="https://images.gr-assets.com/hostedimages/1693740193ra/34680807.gif" width="280" height="180" alt="description" class="gr-hostedUserImg" loading="lazy"><br><br>Ugh, he had a way with words. I know "my lady" might sound like something out of a historical romance novel, but I can't help loving it.<br><br><blockquote>
  <i>
    <b>“You should wear a bell so you don’t give me a heart attack.” <br>“A bell? Like the one’s humans put on their cats?” He tipped his head to the side curiously, seeming to give the idea some thought. “Only if you promise to drag me around on a leash. Then I’ll gladly wear your collar.”</b>
  </i>
</blockquote><br><br>*SCREAMINGGGGG*<br><br><blockquote>
  <i>
    <b>“I don’t understand your anger,” I said. “Would you have preferred she penetrate me instead?” <br>Whispered curses floated through the air. “It certainly would have been preferable.” <br>That was fair enough. I still didn’t understand, but humans worked in mysterious ways. <br>“Well then, we’ll try that next time.” The explosion of cursing that followed my declaration told me perhaps I should stop talking entirely."</b>
  </i>
</blockquote><br><br>He was effortlessly funny, and the best part was that he was completely serious here. <br><br><blockquote>
  <i>
    <b>"Everly is not, and has never been, a mere fascination. She is my reason, my logic. She is my one and only God. Think I’m mad if you wish. There is nothing left for me in this existence except for her, and I would sooner rip myself apart than allow you or any other being to stand in her way."<br><br>“I’ll love you endlessly, darling.”</b>
  </i>
</blockquote><br><br>'Darling' always does it for me.<br><br><blockquote>
  <i>
    <b>“I would build a temple for you if I could,” I said, dragging my nose along her skin as I inhaled. “Lock you away like a gilded goddess and worship at your feet for eternity.”</b>
  </i>
</blockquote><br><br> ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ ‎ <img src="https://images.gr-assets.com/hostedimages/1680758828ra/34114087.gif" width="280" height="180" alt="description" class="gr-hostedUserImg" loading="lazy"><br><br>He was obsessed. Honestly, even the word 'obsessed' feels like an understatement. At first, I was confused when, after just a few minutes of meeting her, he jumped straight to, "Who f*cking hurt you? Give me their name.", but it all made sense once he revealed why he knew who she was. He had been searching for her for centuries — long before she was even born. He had a vision of her, something nearly impossible for demons, where she begged for his help, and that vision drove him to Earth to find her. Imagine your man waiting for you for centuries — a new standard. He even threatened Lucifer himself and was ready to betray Hell for her.<br><br>Now, onto my main issue — sharing. The moment a hero expresses any desire to share the heroine, it's an instant DNF for me. I know this might not bother everyone, but it's something I personally can't stand. I prefer heroes who are so possessive that they would never let another man near you. Don’t get me wrong — there isn’t a single scene like that in this book, but Callum does mention that it wouldn't be a problem for him.<br><br><blockquote>
  <i>
    <b>“I thought you weren’t the jealous type.” <br>“Oh, certainly not, love. I’m very welcoming and generous to guests. It’s intruders I have a problem with.”</b>
  </i>
</blockquote><br><br>Then he started talking about other men "helping him" while he fuck*d her. While I understand that this might not be a problem for everyone, it’s something I personally dislike. I liked Callum enough to continue, but it did leave a bitter taste.<br><br><br>Our heroine, Everly, is next. While I liked her, I didn't feel a strong connection to her character. She was portrayed as a powerful witch learning to control her magic, yet at the same time, she came across as sweet and shy. She was also a virgin who enjoyed reading smutty books. Through her relationship with Callum, she discovered a dominant side in the bedroom, and although I’m completely obsessed with dominant women, the moment she suddenly started talking dirty to him, it somehow caught me off guard.. One moment she was sweet, and then suddenly, "Do you really want it that bad? Such a needy boy." I couldn't quite picture it — I don’t know why but, it felt out of character.<br><br><br>As for the smutty scenes, I have to admit that I enjoyed them, some even a lot. But certain phrases, like 'my puckered hole,' were just a big no for me. I'm sorry, but it’s probably the worst possible way you can describe that.<br><br><br>The second thing that made me lower my rating is that I found myself a bit bored. It’s not that nothing was happening, but usually, I can finish a book of this length in a day—this one took me three.<br><br><br>In the end, I'll just say that while I enjoyed certain aspects of this book, would I recommend it to anyone overall? Unfortunately, no. I wish I could, though.</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/106675807?shelf=2-and-a-half-stars" class="Button Button--tag Button--medium"><span class="Button__labelItem">2-and-a-half-stars</span></a><span tabindex="-1"> </span><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/list/106675807?she